1
00:00:01,102 --> 00:00:04,442
(Love Is Blooming)

2
00:00:04,742 --> 00:00:05,912
Nice to see you all.

3
00:00:07,572 --> 00:00:09,682
- All right. - After last week's preview...

4
00:00:10,642 --> 00:00:12,688
(The sneak peek of Moo chewy cookies was in limelight.)

5
00:00:12,712 --> 00:00:13,851
the comments...

6
00:00:13,852 --> 00:00:15,351
(Chuckling)

7
00:00:15,352 --> 00:00:17,282
They were waiting for this moment.

8
00:00:17,522 --> 00:00:18,881
I'm not sure if it's a positive thing,

9
00:00:18,882 --> 00:00:20,221
- but it did get a lot of attention. - Yes.

10
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:22,662
I wonder how Dubai chewy cookies feel about this, though.

11
00:00:23,962 --> 00:00:25,691
They know their days are numbered.

12
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:27,162
(Muffled laughter)

13
00:00:27,292 --> 00:00:29,892
Is this like a rain-calling ceremony to put an end to this trend?

14
00:00:30,862 --> 00:00:32,771
No. I didn't want to put an end to it.

15
00:00:32,772 --> 00:00:34,172
I wanted to popularize them.

16
00:00:34,232 --> 00:00:37,071
Everyone jokingly said that I was going to end the trend.

17
00:00:37,072 --> 00:00:38,141
But some people reacted differently.

18
00:00:38,142 --> 00:00:39,611
"I'll finally get to have some now."

19
00:00:39,612 --> 00:00:40,641
- Right. - Yes.

20
00:00:40,642 --> 00:00:42,141
- There are always lines. - Right, there are always lines.

21
00:00:42,142 --> 00:00:43,881
They were incredibly hard to come by.

22
00:00:43,882 --> 00:00:44,882
- Right. - Yes.

23
00:00:44,883 --> 00:00:47,011
I want to be able to buy them without having to line up.

24
00:00:47,012 --> 00:00:49,398
So many people I know want to, but they can't because of the lines.

25
00:00:49,422 --> 00:00:51,721
You wanted to unburden the distribution of the cookies.

26
00:00:51,722 --> 00:00:53,392
You tried to stabilize the situation.

27
00:00:53,522 --> 00:00:55,121
I wanted to normalize the distribution status.

28
00:00:55,122 --> 00:00:56,522
So I was deployed on this.

29
00:00:56,762 --> 00:00:58,262
He must be very influential.

30
00:00:58,862 --> 00:01:00,261
It wasn't what I wanted, but I ended up becoming...

31
00:01:00,262 --> 00:01:01,431
Trend Ending Man, not TSM.

32
00:01:01,432 --> 00:01:02,662
Let's take a look.

33
00:01:02,832 --> 00:01:04,501
- TSM. - Trend Ending Man.

34
00:01:04,502 --> 00:01:06,432
- The Death of Trends. - Cutter of Trends.

35
00:01:08,632 --> 00:01:10,271
"Chewy, Sweet, and Nutty."

36
00:01:10,272 --> 00:01:11,832
(Chewy, Sweet, and Nutty, It's Endless)

37
00:01:13,272 --> 00:01:16,212
(Groaning)

38
00:01:17,142 --> 00:01:18,382
(Flicking)

39
00:01:18,812 --> 00:01:19,812
My gosh.

40
00:01:21,182 --> 00:01:22,351
- His pajamas remind me... - His pajamas...

41
00:01:22,352 --> 00:01:23,451
The color looks off.

42
00:01:23,452 --> 00:01:25,452
Of Bunta Takakura's pajamas in "Crayon Shin-chan."

43
00:01:27,752 --> 00:01:29,922
(Half asleep, he walks over to the door.)

44
00:01:31,022 --> 00:01:32,292
(Opening)

45
00:01:32,632 --> 00:01:33,731
(He's opening the door when he just woke up.)

46
00:01:33,732 --> 00:01:35,092
You're going out in those pajamas?

47
00:01:35,902 --> 00:01:37,202
I don't have dementia.

48
00:01:38,802 --> 00:01:40,002
Yes.

49
00:01:45,072 --> 00:01:47,012
- What's that? - What is it?

50
00:01:50,342 --> 00:01:51,742
Is that wine? What is it?

51
00:01:51,912 --> 00:01:53,151
Are they ingredients for DCC?

52
00:01:53,152 --> 00:01:54,352
They look quite fancy.

53
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:56,652
(Yawning)

54
00:01:57,452 --> 00:01:59,221
(Current time, 7:20am)

55
00:01:59,222 --> 00:02:00,522
I woke up too early.

56
00:02:02,292 --> 00:02:04,962
(He's still sleepy, but he quickly opens up the package.)

57
00:02:06,532 --> 00:02:07,632
It's packaged nicely.

58
00:02:09,092 --> 00:02:10,732
Isn't that green onion?

59
00:02:10,932 --> 00:02:12,902
Scallion. Why do you have that?

60
00:02:13,402 --> 00:02:14,602
They look like tulips.

61
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:16,071
(Puzzled)

62
00:02:16,072 --> 00:02:18,772
- They look too green. - So I have to add these?

63
00:02:19,842 --> 00:02:22,072
- Bam. - Bam.

64
00:02:22,742 --> 00:02:23,912
(Bam)

65
00:02:24,112 --> 00:02:25,682
Mr. Deep in the Night,

66
00:02:25,982 --> 00:02:27,281
Kyung Rok, left an impression on me.

67
00:02:27,282 --> 00:02:28,682
So I started to buy flowers.

68
00:02:29,852 --> 00:02:31,921
(Moomoo and Kyung Rok were born in the Year of the Snake, 1977.)

69
00:02:31,922 --> 00:02:33,722
(They recently met on the show.)

70
00:02:34,722 --> 00:02:36,291
(He's as energetic as a man born in the Year of the Snake.)

71
00:02:36,292 --> 00:02:37,322
What a cute guy.

72
00:02:39,192 --> 00:02:41,392
- He looked really happy. - Right.

73
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:42,862
So I wanted to try it too.

74
00:02:44,232 --> 00:02:47,902
I recently found out that there was a flower delivery subscription.

75
00:02:49,802 --> 00:02:51,872
Watching his clips really motivated me.

76
00:02:52,302 --> 00:02:55,642
I signed up to the subscription the day after meeting him.

77
00:02:56,082 --> 00:02:57,411
- Why do you keep trying to buy... - Listen.

78
00:02:57,412 --> 00:02:58,742
Other people's lifestyles?

79
00:02:59,412 --> 00:03:00,452
Right.

80
00:03:01,252 --> 00:03:03,322
How many subscriptions do you hold now?

81
00:03:03,682 --> 00:03:06,092
- I should have about six. - You get your produce delivered.

82
00:03:06,252 --> 00:03:08,972
I started to order imperfect produce after watching Ja Yeon's clips.

83
00:03:09,492 --> 00:03:11,821
The plants covering my wall are from my subscription service.

84
00:03:11,822 --> 00:03:13,302
- That's a subscription too? - It is.

85
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:16,162
I bought flowers after watching Kyung Rok's clips.

86
00:03:16,532 --> 00:03:18,101
- They're all subscription services? - Yes.

87
00:03:18,102 --> 00:03:20,501
- He joined us only for one episode. - Yes.

88
00:03:20,502 --> 00:03:22,662
You stole two things from him, Moomoo Day and flowers.

89
00:03:22,872 --> 00:03:24,371
It went from Kyung Rok Day to Moomoo Day.

90
00:03:24,372 --> 00:03:25,941
I'll go to Jebi Coffee House in the near future.

91
00:03:25,942 --> 00:03:27,672
(Don't let your guard down, Kyung Rok.)

92
00:03:28,842 --> 00:03:30,581
He's scary. He's stealing everything.

93
00:03:30,582 --> 00:03:32,712
(He's stealing everything.)

94
00:03:36,252 --> 00:03:37,981
(Just like Kyung Rok, )

95
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:39,862
(he calmly starts his day by filling the vase.)

96
00:03:41,092 --> 00:03:43,092
(Gingerly placing the fresh flowers in the vase)

97
00:03:43,822 --> 00:03:45,891
- I bought the vase too. - They're not in full bloom.

98
00:03:45,892 --> 00:03:47,132
(Slightly under-bloomed tulips)

99
00:03:48,762 --> 00:03:50,602
They'd look beautiful in a soju bottle.

100
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:52,301
(An honest comment of an expert florist)

101
00:03:52,302 --> 00:03:53,672
(Look at that!)

102
00:03:54,672 --> 00:03:55,802
(Snapping)

103
00:03:56,472 --> 00:03:57,572
Plant Murderer.

104
00:04:01,242 --> 00:04:03,012
(Let me see.)

105
00:04:04,582 --> 00:04:05,982
Why do they look like green onions?

106
00:04:07,482 --> 00:04:09,521
They sent under-bloomed tulips, though.

107
00:04:09,522 --> 00:04:10,821
- I thought they were green onions. - Right. They're so...

108
00:04:10,822 --> 00:04:12,281
- That way, they can last longer. - Right.

109
00:04:12,282 --> 00:04:13,791
But why did you buy tulips out of all flowers?

110
00:04:13,792 --> 00:04:15,421
- There are many flowers. - I like tulips.

111
00:04:15,422 --> 00:04:16,422
- Why? - The color...

112
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:17,521
- They look the prettiest... - They come in many colors.

113
00:04:17,522 --> 00:04:18,822
And have various colors.

114
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:20,631
Once they bloom, their petals...

115
00:04:20,632 --> 00:04:22,461
- open up like this. - Right.

116
00:04:22,462 --> 00:04:23,962
They're beautiful at every stage.

117
00:04:24,202 --> 00:04:26,301
- Even their buds are beautiful. - They look like fruits.

118
00:04:26,302 --> 00:04:29,341
Does this mean you're ending the tulip's popularity too?

119
00:04:29,342 --> 00:04:31,001
This will even affect the Netherlands.

120
00:04:31,002 --> 00:04:32,101
(It could gain international traction.)

121
00:04:32,102 --> 00:04:34,371
- Even abroad? - It will have a major impact.

122
00:04:34,372 --> 00:04:35,441
(Impressed)

123
00:04:35,442 --> 00:04:36,742
Hiddink will be surprised.

124
00:04:37,382 --> 00:04:39,222
(Moomoo's influence is on a global scale now.)

125
00:04:39,852 --> 00:04:41,982
(He quickly opens up the next box of flowers.)

126
00:04:43,282 --> 00:04:44,651
(Inhaling sharply)

127
00:04:44,652 --> 00:04:46,452
Will they grow taller?

128
00:04:46,722 --> 00:04:48,922
They look exactly the same. And they're the same color.

129
00:04:49,622 --> 00:04:50,991
- What's that? - They're the same ones.

130
00:04:50,992 --> 00:04:52,892
(They're the green-onion-looking tulips again.)

131
00:04:53,392 --> 00:04:55,532
(Tilting his head)

132
00:04:57,762 --> 00:04:59,001
They do look like green onions.

133
00:04:59,002 --> 00:05:00,302
Right. They're very aromatic.

134
00:05:04,172 --> 00:05:05,742
These ones will look different, right?

135
00:05:06,442 --> 00:05:08,041
(Opening up the third box of flowers with hope)

136
00:05:08,042 --> 00:05:09,322
They all look the same. Darn it.

137
00:05:11,142 --> 00:05:12,812
- The petals are all closed. - Yes.

138
00:05:13,082 --> 00:05:15,358
I ordered them in different colors, but they look all the same.

139
00:05:15,382 --> 00:05:16,422
Right.

140
00:05:17,252 --> 00:05:19,252
I remember ordering them in different colors.

141
00:05:19,622 --> 00:05:20,952
They just look like scallions.

142
00:05:21,892 --> 00:05:22,991
They're scallions.

143
00:05:22,992 --> 00:05:24,421
(A new trend, green onion delivery subscription)

144
00:05:24,422 --> 00:05:26,568
It felt like I just bought three bunches of scallions.

145
00:05:26,592 --> 00:05:28,561
I bet they will taste great if we make fresh kimchi with them.

146
00:05:28,562 --> 00:05:31,161
(I bet they will taste great if we make fresh kimchi with them.)

147
00:05:31,162 --> 00:05:33,371
- Make fresh kimchi with them? - Make fresh kimchi with them.

148
00:05:33,372 --> 00:05:35,032
(Make fresh tulip kimchi)

149
00:05:35,332 --> 00:05:36,902
- It's tasty with ramyeon too. - Yes.

150
00:05:37,372 --> 00:05:38,402
Totally.

151
00:05:40,772 --> 00:05:42,112
(Careful)

152
00:05:44,242 --> 00:05:46,162
(Placing the fresh green onion with utmost care)

153
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:49,532
- But once they bloom... - They look beautiful.

154
00:05:50,322 --> 00:05:51,581
They will bloom beautifully, right?

155
00:05:51,582 --> 00:05:52,752
(Scratching)

156
00:05:54,392 --> 00:05:56,922
(He may be different from his friend, Kyung Rok, but...)

157
00:05:57,722 --> 00:05:59,591
Kyung Rok has a prettier vase.

158
00:05:59,592 --> 00:06:01,061
- Here's why, Hyun Moo. - Okay.

159
00:06:01,062 --> 00:06:02,691
- You bought those vases together. - Right.

160
00:06:02,692 --> 00:06:04,161
- But he bought them separately... - Right.

161
00:06:04,162 --> 00:06:06,071
For different reasons and stories.

162
00:06:06,072 --> 00:06:07,232
I didn't have time.

163
00:06:07,902 --> 00:06:09,601
- It's like I'm on the run. - You were in a hurry.

164
00:06:09,602 --> 00:06:10,871
I had to catch up like I was being chased.

165
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:13,153
You should have bought the vases on a subscription basis.

166
00:06:13,572 --> 00:06:14,671
- Is there such a service? - They'll come inside?

167
00:06:14,672 --> 00:06:16,112
You must change the water every day.

168
00:06:16,812 --> 00:06:18,358
That way, you can delay them from withering away.

169
00:06:18,382 --> 00:06:20,682
Sign up to a water-changing subscription.

170
00:06:21,112 --> 00:06:23,182
I'll have 50 visitors a day then.

171
00:06:24,752 --> 00:06:26,622
(A popular spot)

172
00:06:27,352 --> 00:06:30,962
(Placing the soon-to-bloom tulips around his house)

173
00:06:32,662 --> 00:06:35,932
(Trudging along)

174
00:06:37,402 --> 00:06:40,071
(He quietly pulls out the exercise machine.)

175
00:06:40,072 --> 00:06:41,931
- I exercise every morning. - The indoor bicycle.

176
00:06:41,932 --> 00:06:44,171
- You should exercise. - You use the machine regularly.

177
00:06:44,172 --> 00:06:47,142
- It's my morning routine. - Okay.

178
00:06:47,842 --> 00:06:49,272
(Let me see.)

179
00:06:50,512 --> 00:06:52,182
(Choose your workout.)

180
00:06:54,112 --> 00:06:55,112
(Get ready.)

181
00:07:00,852 --> 00:07:03,292
(He starts pedaling with ease.)

182
00:07:04,062 --> 00:07:05,762
(Just like my friend Kyung Rok...)

183
00:07:06,262 --> 00:07:07,962
I should start my day with a good sweat.

184
00:07:09,332 --> 00:07:11,532
I can engage my core muscles since there's no handle.

185
00:07:12,832 --> 00:07:16,032
(Let's get this workout started.)

186
00:07:16,372 --> 00:07:18,602
I'll just do it for ten minutes. Let's go.

187
00:07:19,302 --> 00:07:20,941
- I used it so much that... - You should wake up.

188
00:07:20,942 --> 00:07:23,281
I ended up replacing the seat.

189
00:07:23,282 --> 00:07:24,982
- Really? - I rode it so much.

190
00:07:25,282 --> 00:07:26,411
- You didn't buy a new one. - I'm almost there.

191
00:07:26,412 --> 00:07:27,652
But you only replaced the seat.

192
00:07:27,982 --> 00:07:29,282
(Laughing)

193
00:07:29,852 --> 00:07:30,852
Stop.

194
00:07:30,853 --> 00:07:31,921
(Finally!)

195
00:07:31,922 --> 00:07:33,322
(Panting)

196
00:07:35,752 --> 00:07:37,261
It's nice that I can record my workouts.

197
00:07:37,262 --> 00:07:39,262
- Ten minutes. - I do it for at least ten minutes.

198
00:07:40,132 --> 00:07:41,761
- Look at you sweating. - I'm sweating so much.

199
00:07:41,762 --> 00:07:42,762
(He's sweating profusely.)

200
00:07:42,763 --> 00:07:44,843
- I got sweaty just in ten minutes. - Gosh, I'm hot.

201
00:07:45,702 --> 00:07:47,832
(He perfectly completes his morning routine.)

202
00:07:49,472 --> 00:07:52,302
(Washing his hands thoroughly with cool water)

203
00:07:53,612 --> 00:07:54,771
(Rolling up his sleeves)

204
00:07:54,772 --> 00:07:55,942
(I don't have time to rest.)

205
00:07:56,612 --> 00:07:58,541
(Uncovering)

206
00:07:58,542 --> 00:07:59,752
I got a lot done yesterday.

207
00:08:00,012 --> 00:08:01,152
What's that?

208
00:08:01,382 --> 00:08:02,751
(He's tired, but he uncovers something.)

209
00:08:02,752 --> 00:08:04,022
- What's that? - What?

210
00:08:05,282 --> 00:08:07,602
I did that for two hours last night before going to sleep.

211
00:08:09,222 --> 00:08:10,862
- Are they pistachios? - What is that?

212
00:08:11,792 --> 00:08:13,752
- Are they peanuts? What are those? - Pistachios.

213
00:08:13,932 --> 00:08:15,172
- What are those? - Pistachios.

214
00:08:15,792 --> 00:08:18,002
As you all wished, I will finally make...

215
00:08:18,602 --> 00:08:19,801
Moo chewy cookies.

216
00:08:19,802 --> 00:08:21,101
Watch your six, Dubai chewy cookies.

217
00:08:21,102 --> 00:08:23,201
(Moo chewy cookies)

218
00:08:23,202 --> 00:08:25,041
Dubai chewy cookies,

219
00:08:25,042 --> 00:08:27,772
also known as DCC, are gaining a lot of popularity.

220
00:08:28,672 --> 00:08:30,511
(The whole country is crazy for DCC.)

221
00:08:30,512 --> 00:08:31,512
Right.

222
00:08:31,982 --> 00:08:34,282
(It's chewy on the outside and crispy on the inside.)

223
00:08:35,152 --> 00:08:36,681
(Due to its addictive flavor, its popularity shows...)

224
00:08:36,682 --> 00:08:37,982
(no signs of slowing down.)

225
00:08:38,882 --> 00:08:41,251
(Bum June visited Yoo Ji Tae with precious Dubai chewy cookies.)

226
00:08:41,252 --> 00:08:42,592
(Envious)

227
00:08:43,192 --> 00:08:44,521
Things were great back then.

228
00:08:44,522 --> 00:08:46,092
Things were great back then?

229
00:08:46,832 --> 00:08:49,392
(TSM Hyun Moo received endless requests from DCC lovers.)

230
00:08:49,632 --> 00:08:50,831
(When will you make Dubai chewy cookies?)

231
00:08:50,832 --> 00:08:52,152
(Let's put an end to this trend.)

232
00:08:52,932 --> 00:08:54,401
(Dubai chewy cookies think they're all that.)

233
00:08:54,402 --> 00:08:56,742
(They're super snobby.)

234
00:08:57,442 --> 00:09:00,272
(Dubai chewy cookies' popularity is skyrocketing.)

235
00:09:00,572 --> 00:09:01,611
They're always sold out.

236
00:09:01,612 --> 00:09:03,781
You always have to wait for them for 1 or 2 hours.

237
00:09:03,782 --> 00:09:05,318
- Right. - That leaves me with no choice.

238
00:09:05,342 --> 00:09:07,012
I should make my own Dubai chewy cookies.

239
00:09:07,352 --> 00:09:09,511
I decided to make them because I wanted them.

240
00:09:09,512 --> 00:09:11,351
Remember leng saeb?

241
00:09:11,352 --> 00:09:12,521
Oh, leng saeb.

242
00:09:12,522 --> 00:09:13,728
(He cooked it while making a scene.)

243
00:09:13,752 --> 00:09:16,322
I had to cook my own leng saeb because I couldn't find it.

244
00:09:16,822 --> 00:09:20,432
To make authentic Dubai chewy cookies,

245
00:09:20,632 --> 00:09:22,592
I'm going to start from scratch.

246
00:09:22,832 --> 00:09:25,362
To make Dubai chewy cookies, I have to start...

247
00:09:25,502 --> 00:09:28,572
by roasting pistachios and blending them.

248
00:09:29,202 --> 00:09:30,771
That painting is getting on my nerves.

249
00:09:30,772 --> 00:09:31,971
(Laughing)

250
00:09:31,972 --> 00:09:33,042
It's annoying, right?

251
00:09:33,172 --> 00:09:36,812
Then the wrapper is made with marshmallows and butter.

252
00:09:37,042 --> 00:09:38,481
I must melt them on low heat.

253
00:09:38,482 --> 00:09:40,802
Once they melt, I add cocoa powder and dried whole milk...

254
00:09:41,052 --> 00:09:43,321
to get that chocolatey color. That's how you make the wrapper.

255
00:09:43,322 --> 00:09:45,252
Afterward, you wrap the filling up with it.

256
00:09:45,722 --> 00:09:47,492
Then Dubai chewy cookies are ready.

257
00:09:47,822 --> 00:09:51,721
Anyway, I'm putting my reputation on the line for this.

258
00:09:51,722 --> 00:09:54,732
When my reputation is at stake, I always do a painting about it.

259
00:09:55,132 --> 00:09:56,632
You see,

260
00:09:57,102 --> 00:09:58,662
I'm the magician of colors.

261
00:09:59,362 --> 00:10:01,731
It wasn't hard to paint this.

262
00:10:01,732 --> 00:10:02,772
I can see that.

263
00:10:02,972 --> 00:10:04,941
Don't cheap Dubai chewy cookies have that color?

264
00:10:04,942 --> 00:10:06,042
- Fake ones. - Right?

265
00:10:06,672 --> 00:10:07,772
Ta-da.

266
00:10:08,442 --> 00:10:10,041
You can't execute this kind of color...

267
00:10:10,042 --> 00:10:11,682
by following science or calculating math.

268
00:10:11,982 --> 00:10:13,051
I was in my moment.

269
00:10:13,052 --> 00:10:15,082
- "Ta-da." - Look at his hand movements.

270
00:10:15,612 --> 00:10:17,752
I don't know how I ended up with this color.

271
00:10:17,982 --> 00:10:19,992
It's all in my instincts.

272
00:10:20,292 --> 00:10:22,922
It's not something that can be taught.

273
00:10:23,992 --> 00:10:24,992
(It's me, Moo chewy cookies.)

274
00:10:24,993 --> 00:10:26,432
(Shutting his eyes)

275
00:10:26,562 --> 00:10:28,432
I still have his first painting.

276
00:10:28,962 --> 00:10:30,762
The value of that painting must be crazy.

277
00:10:31,632 --> 00:10:33,152
I can no longer paint with that style.

278
00:10:33,202 --> 00:10:34,371
- No, right? - No.

279
00:10:34,372 --> 00:10:36,101
- Your style has become too simple. - Right.

280
00:10:36,102 --> 00:10:38,771
- Right, it's simple. - Back then, you used many colors.

281
00:10:38,772 --> 00:10:41,241
For CODE KUNST's portrait painting, I took my time with it.

282
00:10:41,242 --> 00:10:42,441
Also, I took breaks.

283
00:10:42,442 --> 00:10:43,881
But now, it seems I paint for commercial purposes.

284
00:10:43,882 --> 00:10:44,912
Commercial purposes.

285
00:10:45,012 --> 00:10:47,288
- I have no time, so it got simple. - You don't have time.

286
00:10:47,312 --> 00:10:48,412
Your style became simple.

287
00:10:48,482 --> 00:10:50,852
You know what? Your art is falling into a pattern too.

288
00:10:51,422 --> 00:10:54,452
- And his current paintings... - That's more annoying.

289
00:10:54,652 --> 00:10:56,821
- are symmetrical these days. - Right.

290
00:10:56,822 --> 00:10:58,391
- When he started, they weren't. - Yes.

291
00:10:58,392 --> 00:10:59,691
- They became repetitive. - That kind of thing happens...

292
00:10:59,692 --> 00:11:00,991
- after 20 years of painting. - I mean,

293
00:11:00,992 --> 00:11:03,201
- he's trying to be professional. - I didn't have time back then.

294
00:11:03,202 --> 00:11:05,522
His works became repetitive after drawing a few paintings.

295
00:11:05,762 --> 00:11:07,042
I must break out of my patterns.

296
00:11:07,932 --> 00:11:09,331
(He's diligently opening pistachios for Moo chewy cookies.)

297
00:11:09,332 --> 00:11:10,802
Let's make them the authentic way.

298
00:11:11,742 --> 00:11:14,112
Opening them is the first step of Dubai chewy cookies.

299
00:11:15,342 --> 00:11:17,862
I came home after work last night and then opened half of them.

300
00:11:17,942 --> 00:11:19,241
You did that yourself?

301
00:11:19,242 --> 00:11:20,481
They didn't sell shelled ones.

302
00:11:20,482 --> 00:11:22,111
So you had to open them yourself? All by hand?

303
00:11:22,112 --> 00:11:23,622
It took me two weeks to get them.

304
00:11:23,922 --> 00:11:25,551
- There are shelled ones. - Because there are no pistachios?

305
00:11:25,552 --> 00:11:26,621
- Right. - But it's impossible to buy them.

306
00:11:26,622 --> 00:11:28,621
Actually, I wanted to make them too.

307
00:11:28,622 --> 00:11:31,421
So I asked my sister for some pistachios.

308
00:11:31,422 --> 00:11:33,191
- She couldn't find them... - She couldn't find them either?

309
00:11:33,192 --> 00:11:34,752
- even for her bakery. - No pistachios?

310
00:11:34,932 --> 00:11:37,061
It's hard to get other ingredients that go into these cookies...

311
00:11:37,062 --> 00:11:38,131
like cocoa powder...

312
00:11:38,132 --> 00:11:39,201
- and white chocolate. - Right.

313
00:11:39,202 --> 00:11:40,642
So she couldn't make other products.

314
00:11:41,372 --> 00:11:42,631
But you couldn't find that thing?

315
00:11:42,632 --> 00:11:45,141
- What? - The green jam or something.

316
00:11:45,142 --> 00:11:46,201
- The spread? - The spread.

317
00:11:46,202 --> 00:11:47,362
That's not the authentic way.

318
00:11:47,942 --> 00:11:49,942
- I'm going legit with this. - Right.

319
00:11:50,382 --> 00:11:52,481
- But it's way tastier that way. - It's good.

320
00:11:52,482 --> 00:11:55,012
- You're making it from scratch. - Exactly.

321
00:11:57,052 --> 00:11:59,582
I still have so many to open. When will I finish?

322
00:12:00,192 --> 00:12:02,392
(Oh, no.)

323
00:12:03,792 --> 00:12:05,391
That's a lot of pistachios.

324
00:12:05,392 --> 00:12:08,732
- So 1 bag weighs about 1.36kg. - My gosh.

325
00:12:08,992 --> 00:12:10,062
Just one bag?

326
00:12:10,602 --> 00:12:12,322
Opening them is really tough on your hands.

327
00:12:12,862 --> 00:12:14,332
This part hurt like crazy.

328
00:12:15,302 --> 00:12:16,471
(He focuses on cracking them open while bearing the pain.)

329
00:12:16,472 --> 00:12:17,772
There are always stubborn ones.

330
00:12:18,402 --> 00:12:19,571
(Wincing)

331
00:12:19,572 --> 00:12:20,912
You look cute like a chipmunk.

332
00:12:21,212 --> 00:12:22,342
You keep cracking them open.

333
00:12:23,212 --> 00:12:24,581
He's mocking Hyun Moo.

334
00:12:24,582 --> 00:12:25,582
(Laughing)

335
00:12:25,912 --> 00:12:28,982
You look like an old squirrel.

336
00:12:29,782 --> 00:12:32,121
- Get rid of "old." - I see them a lot in Gwacheon.

337
00:12:32,122 --> 00:12:33,691
- Right. - Oh, those clumsy ones?

338
00:12:33,692 --> 00:12:36,291
- Yes. - Those squirrels with dry fur.

339
00:12:36,292 --> 00:12:38,262
(Chip-Moo keeps shelling the pistachios.)

340
00:12:38,462 --> 00:12:39,691
You're saying lovely things about me.

341
00:12:39,692 --> 00:12:41,652
(Chip-Moo is working hard to make authentic DCC.)

342
00:12:42,662 --> 00:12:45,762
(Crackling)

343
00:12:47,172 --> 00:12:49,202
You look so lonely because you're doing it alone.

344
00:12:50,442 --> 00:12:52,142
(Looking over)

345
00:12:52,972 --> 00:12:55,842
(He stops shelling and gets up.)

346
00:12:57,642 --> 00:13:00,211
(He takes out an LP record to break the silence.)

347
00:13:00,212 --> 00:13:01,282
Seriously.

348
00:13:02,412 --> 00:13:04,721
- Seriously. - I was inspired by Kyung Rok.

349
00:13:04,722 --> 00:13:06,898
(Listening to the record just like his friend, Kyung Rok)

350
00:13:06,922 --> 00:13:08,552
It was too lonely and quiet.

351
00:13:08,752 --> 00:13:10,022
Playing music is good.

352
00:13:12,562 --> 00:13:15,432
(The music resonates throughout the quiet house.)

353
00:13:16,732 --> 00:13:18,901
(He starts shelling pistachios again...)

354
00:13:18,902 --> 00:13:21,032
(as classical music fills the silence.)

355
00:13:24,042 --> 00:13:26,372
(The table is getting messier.)

356
00:13:27,642 --> 00:13:29,942
(And the record player is spinning.)

357
00:13:30,742 --> 00:13:33,482
(And there seems to be no end to the world of pistachios.)

358
00:13:34,682 --> 00:13:35,911
(Moomoo reaches the stage where he begins to enjoy the work.)

359
00:13:35,912 --> 00:13:37,352
That must've been a lot of work.

360
00:13:37,682 --> 00:13:39,891
Once you keep shelling them, you stop thinking.

361
00:13:39,892 --> 00:13:40,921
- Right. - You start spacing out.

362
00:13:40,922 --> 00:13:42,402
- People space out. - They space out.

363
00:13:42,792 --> 00:13:44,521
(There are still a lot of pistachios left.)

364
00:13:44,522 --> 00:13:45,998
You should do that kind of work in the morning.

365
00:13:46,022 --> 00:13:47,931
- Strangely, you get lazy at night. - Right.

366
00:13:47,932 --> 00:13:48,961
That's so true.

367
00:13:48,962 --> 00:13:51,361
(For authentic Dubai chewy cookies...)

368
00:13:51,362 --> 00:13:52,502
I look like a master.

369
00:13:52,862 --> 00:13:54,131
(Pistachio shells begin to pile up.)

370
00:13:54,132 --> 00:13:55,971
Your house looks quite fancy with the flowers.

371
00:13:55,972 --> 00:13:56,972
- Right? - Yes.

372
00:13:56,973 --> 00:13:58,101
Don't I look like a grandpa from the Netherlands?

373
00:13:58,102 --> 00:14:00,211
(The grandpa from the Netherlands is hooked on pistachios.)

374
00:14:00,212 --> 00:14:01,312
He's calling himself old.

375
00:14:01,912 --> 00:14:03,341
(Blasting)

376
00:14:03,342 --> 00:14:04,411
"Ba-dum."

377
00:14:04,412 --> 00:14:05,412
I'm done!

378
00:14:05,413 --> 00:14:07,582
(I'm done too!)

379
00:14:08,382 --> 00:14:11,852
(This should be enough.)

380
00:14:12,982 --> 00:14:15,452
(He finally escapes from the pistachio abyss.)

381
00:14:16,692 --> 00:14:18,191
(He checks the recipe before he starts making them.)

382
00:14:18,192 --> 00:14:20,092
I need 640g of pistachios.

383
00:14:20,732 --> 00:14:23,131
- The recipe is that long? - Of course.

384
00:14:23,132 --> 00:14:24,601
A lot of effort goes into making them.

385
00:14:24,602 --> 00:14:26,461
- They're expensive for a reason. - There's a reason for it.

386
00:14:26,462 --> 00:14:27,862
Making them requires a lot of work.

387
00:14:28,172 --> 00:14:29,372
Where did you get the recipe?

388
00:14:29,702 --> 00:14:31,201
I looked up a lot of recipes.

389
00:14:31,202 --> 00:14:34,372
I chose the recipe that used raw ingredients from scratch.

390
00:14:34,912 --> 00:14:36,472
Not the recipe with pistachio spread...

391
00:14:36,612 --> 00:14:37,811
or kadayif.

392
00:14:37,812 --> 00:14:39,641
There was a recipe with only raw ingredients.

393
00:14:39,642 --> 00:14:40,711
I chose that one.

394
00:14:40,712 --> 00:14:41,752
(The recipe from scratch)

395
00:14:42,412 --> 00:14:43,951
I need 640g.

396
00:14:43,952 --> 00:14:45,321
(He starts by weighing the shelled pistachios.)

397
00:14:45,322 --> 00:14:47,092
You need 640g? Isn't that a lot?

398
00:14:47,192 --> 00:14:49,822
A lot of people wanted it, so I'll give them out as presents.

399
00:14:49,922 --> 00:14:50,961
I see. As presents.

400
00:14:50,962 --> 00:14:54,191
I thought about making 20 to 30 cookies.

401
00:14:54,192 --> 00:14:55,732
(The recipe yields about 30 cookies.)

402
00:14:57,332 --> 00:14:59,732
(Pouring)

403
00:15:00,872 --> 00:15:03,072
What? Did I shell too many?

404
00:15:04,042 --> 00:15:05,572
The maximum weight is 1,000g.

405
00:15:06,712 --> 00:15:08,111
I had a lot of leftover pistachios.

406
00:15:08,112 --> 00:15:09,792
(It went over the maximum weight, 1,000g.)

407
00:15:10,842 --> 00:15:13,352
(Leaving only the amount of pistachios he needs)

408
00:15:14,552 --> 00:15:15,712
That's 640g.

409
00:15:16,822 --> 00:15:18,551
You should've used them all. Why did you leave some?

410
00:15:18,552 --> 00:15:19,752
I needed to follow the recipe.

411
00:15:19,882 --> 00:15:20,921
I didn't want the flavor to change.

412
00:15:20,922 --> 00:15:23,051
That's what I heard. When it comes to baking,

413
00:15:23,052 --> 00:15:24,321
- you must measure precisely. - Right.

414
00:15:24,322 --> 00:15:27,291
- Even 1g can change the flavors. - You need to be precise.

415
00:15:27,292 --> 00:15:29,612
(The difference of 1g could change the outcome in baking.)

416
00:15:30,102 --> 00:15:32,031
"Roast them in the preheated oven for 5 to 7 minutes."

417
00:15:32,032 --> 00:15:35,102
(Roast 640g of pistachios at 150°C for 5 to 7 minutes.)

418
00:15:36,842 --> 00:15:40,212
My gosh, seriously. This is a great oven.

419
00:15:41,712 --> 00:15:42,712
(Opening)

420
00:15:42,713 --> 00:15:43,882
That's my oven.

421
00:15:44,512 --> 00:15:45,841
You said you'd take it later.

422
00:15:45,842 --> 00:15:47,151
- Right. He said that. - I asked him to store it for me...

423
00:15:47,152 --> 00:15:48,392
until I made some space for it.

424
00:15:48,612 --> 00:15:50,852
- That's Venix, right? - That's right. How did you know?

425
00:15:50,982 --> 00:15:52,681
My sister uses an oven from Unox.

426
00:15:52,682 --> 00:15:54,121
- That's a higher grade. - Right.

427
00:15:54,122 --> 00:15:57,891
That's why I was eyeing that oven too.

428
00:15:57,892 --> 00:15:59,022
Then he bought it from you.

429
00:15:59,992 --> 00:16:01,991
- That's a great oven. - Right.

430
00:16:01,992 --> 00:16:03,462
(You know its value.)

431
00:16:04,432 --> 00:16:05,762
Before I give it to Jae Hyeon,

432
00:16:06,802 --> 00:16:08,101
I should make sure it's working.

433
00:16:08,102 --> 00:16:09,132
Right.

434
00:16:09,532 --> 00:16:12,072
I'm going to give him everything. They all cost money to buy.

435
00:16:12,542 --> 00:16:13,842
It's all money.

436
00:16:17,982 --> 00:16:20,482
(Pouring out the shelled pistachios)

437
00:16:21,212 --> 00:16:22,711
I should have preheated the oven.

438
00:16:22,712 --> 00:16:23,722
(Oh, right!)

439
00:16:25,082 --> 00:16:27,992
(The pistachios are on standby for now.)

440
00:16:29,052 --> 00:16:30,822
(Turning on the oven)

441
00:16:32,222 --> 00:16:33,321
(The current temperature is at 22°C.)

442
00:16:33,322 --> 00:16:35,431
The oven should be preheated at 150°C.

443
00:16:35,432 --> 00:16:38,032
Roast them to 5 to 7 minutes.

444
00:16:38,332 --> 00:16:41,232
It doesn't take long for the oven to heat up.

445
00:16:41,472 --> 00:16:42,502
Right.

446
00:16:44,202 --> 00:16:48,012
(Heat up fast.)

447
00:16:51,682 --> 00:16:52,982
- My gosh. - What happened?

448
00:16:53,042 --> 00:16:54,152
Please. No!

449
00:16:56,482 --> 00:16:57,621
Please. No!

450
00:16:57,622 --> 00:16:59,852
(It's a disaster.)

451
00:17:00,692 --> 00:17:02,012
I hate it! What's he going to do?

452
00:17:02,552 --> 00:17:03,622
I'm stressed.

453
00:17:03,892 --> 00:17:05,322
(Chuckling)

454
00:17:06,222 --> 00:17:08,292
(Thud)

455
00:17:14,502 --> 00:17:15,901
(Pain-causing cookies)

456
00:17:15,902 --> 00:17:18,302
"Pain-causing cookies."

457
00:17:18,842 --> 00:17:20,871
My gosh. Come on.

458
00:17:20,872 --> 00:17:22,442
(Plopping down)

459
00:17:25,982 --> 00:17:28,452
Oh, dear. Come on.

460
00:17:30,182 --> 00:17:33,151
(It's a huge mess.)

461
00:17:33,152 --> 00:17:34,152
When your anger is on another level,

462
00:17:34,153 --> 00:17:35,928
- you don't even feel angry. - Totally. I know what you mean.

463
00:17:35,952 --> 00:17:38,761
- I'd be so speechless. - I bet you wanted to crush them.

464
00:17:38,762 --> 00:17:41,031
- "Darn it. I won't do it." - I had no energy to curse even.

465
00:17:41,032 --> 00:17:42,032
(He even loses the will to get angry.)

466
00:17:42,033 --> 00:17:43,661
- You can't even laugh about it. - I was so annoyed.

467
00:17:43,662 --> 00:17:44,832
You have a long way to go.

468
00:17:47,102 --> 00:17:51,572
(There's a long way ahead of me.)

469
00:17:52,472 --> 00:17:55,971
Darn it. I can't use them since they're so dirty.

470
00:17:55,972 --> 00:17:57,041
(Covered in dust)

471
00:17:57,042 --> 00:17:58,982
I should shell some more. Darn it.

472
00:18:00,082 --> 00:18:02,611
There goes my entire morning!

473
00:18:02,612 --> 00:18:03,652
(Feeling dejected)

474
00:18:05,222 --> 00:18:06,482
Darn it.

475
00:18:07,092 --> 00:18:08,522
I just hated myself.

476
00:18:09,452 --> 00:18:11,722
I didn't even get angry because I felt so pathetic.

477
00:18:12,392 --> 00:18:13,732
I didn't even want to talk...

478
00:18:14,232 --> 00:18:15,292
to myself.

479
00:18:15,962 --> 00:18:18,132
Here's why. When I was placing the tray down,

480
00:18:18,762 --> 00:18:20,572
I noticed that it was slightly slanted.

481
00:18:21,632 --> 00:18:22,731
It was slightly slanted.

482
00:18:22,732 --> 00:18:23,741
- Right. - My gosh.

483
00:18:23,742 --> 00:18:25,501
- That looks unstable. - I didn't think it would fall.

484
00:18:25,502 --> 00:18:26,741
- Right. - That looks so unstable.

485
00:18:26,742 --> 00:18:28,211
I was so dispirited.

486
00:18:28,212 --> 00:18:30,442
(An overlooked moment led to disaster.)

487
00:18:30,682 --> 00:18:31,742
This is so stressful.

488
00:18:32,382 --> 00:18:33,612
The chipmunk is in despair.

489
00:18:35,882 --> 00:18:38,952
(Chip Moo is in a state of dejection.)

490
00:18:40,052 --> 00:18:41,821
(I should clean them up quickly.)

491
00:18:41,822 --> 00:18:43,322
My gosh. Darn it.

492
00:18:43,862 --> 00:18:45,622
- He can't even blame other people. - Right.

493
00:18:45,692 --> 00:18:47,191
- He caused it. - Right.

494
00:18:47,192 --> 00:18:49,232
(The more he thinks about it, the angrier he gets.)

495
00:18:50,092 --> 00:18:51,232
Darn it.

496
00:18:51,462 --> 00:18:53,502
(Crunchy)

497
00:18:53,902 --> 00:18:55,032
I should eat them.

498
00:18:55,832 --> 00:18:57,141
Darn it.

499
00:18:57,142 --> 00:18:59,202
(Scowling)

500
00:19:02,372 --> 00:19:04,842
I bet his entire house is covered in pistachios.

501
00:19:04,942 --> 00:19:06,082
My house smelled nutty.

502
00:19:08,682 --> 00:19:10,252
(Sighing)

503
00:19:10,782 --> 00:19:12,022
(Grabbing)

504
00:19:12,682 --> 00:19:14,521
(Laughing)

505
00:19:14,522 --> 00:19:15,692
He's annoyed.

506
00:19:16,622 --> 00:19:18,091
Stop falling off the tray. Please.

507
00:19:18,092 --> 00:19:19,322
(Pleading)

508
00:19:20,022 --> 00:19:22,191
(Falling out)

509
00:19:22,192 --> 00:19:23,492
(Sighing)

510
00:19:25,632 --> 00:19:28,602
(Measuring the pistachios he put aside earlier)

511
00:19:31,372 --> 00:19:33,041
(Without the bowl, they weigh 395g.)

512
00:19:33,042 --> 00:19:34,811
(Tapping)

513
00:19:34,812 --> 00:19:36,912
I need to shell 245g more.

514
00:19:38,742 --> 00:19:41,151
- You need to shell a little more. - A little more. Just 245g more.

515
00:19:41,152 --> 00:19:42,381
(He needs 245g more due to the mistake.)

516
00:19:42,382 --> 00:19:43,382
I would've used them...

517
00:19:43,382 --> 00:19:44,382
- if they were for me. - Right.

518
00:19:44,383 --> 00:19:45,781
- But they were for other people. - They were presents for others.

519
00:19:45,782 --> 00:19:46,952
- That's iffy. - Seriously.

520
00:19:47,222 --> 00:19:49,952
You could have rinsed them in water and roasted them.

521
00:19:50,022 --> 00:19:51,661
- Really? You should've told me. - Yes.

522
00:19:51,662 --> 00:19:53,791
- Just wipe them... - He didn't know this happened.

523
00:19:53,792 --> 00:19:56,262
(He didn't know. How could he?)

524
00:19:58,002 --> 00:20:00,031
(Watching)

525
00:20:00,032 --> 00:20:01,471
It's increasing by 1g.

526
00:20:01,472 --> 00:20:02,532
Per pistachio?

527
00:20:03,172 --> 00:20:05,301
- Come on. - Oh, no.

528
00:20:05,302 --> 00:20:07,102
(Each pistachio weighs 1g without the shell.)

529
00:20:08,142 --> 00:20:09,671
I still have to shell a lot more.

530
00:20:09,672 --> 00:20:11,588
(It turned out 640g of pistachios meant shelling 640.)

531
00:20:11,612 --> 00:20:13,751
You have to be that precise about the weight?

532
00:20:13,752 --> 00:20:16,352
It was my first time. I wanted to follow the recipe exactly.

533
00:20:19,882 --> 00:20:21,802
It's increasing slowly like the calories I burn.

534
00:20:22,252 --> 00:20:24,452
- Right. That's a good analogy. - I can relate to that.

535
00:20:25,792 --> 00:20:27,192
It will take forever to shell them.

536
00:20:28,832 --> 00:20:32,002
(It's already been 50 minutes since he started.)

537
00:20:34,532 --> 00:20:36,331
(Step 1: Shell pistachios.)

538
00:20:36,332 --> 00:20:37,341
(He's still on the 1st step.)

539
00:20:37,342 --> 00:20:39,141
You haven't gotten past the first step.

540
00:20:39,142 --> 00:20:40,372
Gosh, seriously.

541
00:20:43,072 --> 00:20:44,111
Why did I eat that?

542
00:20:44,112 --> 00:20:45,782
(I don't want to waste it.)

543
00:20:48,612 --> 00:20:50,321
Gosh, this is a lot.

544
00:20:50,322 --> 00:20:51,521
They're expensive for a reason.

545
00:20:51,522 --> 00:20:53,281
They're expensive for a reason. It requires so much work.

546
00:20:53,282 --> 00:20:54,522
It really does.

547
00:20:54,922 --> 00:20:55,992
I'm almost there.

548
00:20:57,162 --> 00:20:59,562
Okay. One more gram.

549
00:21:00,192 --> 00:21:01,261
(Dropping)

550
00:21:01,262 --> 00:21:02,332
There you go!

551
00:21:03,032 --> 00:21:04,902
- I got it. - He finished.

552
00:21:06,802 --> 00:21:08,501
(He escapes from the pistachio abyss after much effort.)

553
00:21:08,502 --> 00:21:09,532
Good.

554
00:21:10,602 --> 00:21:13,672
I shelled enough for 60 servings.

555
00:21:14,212 --> 00:21:16,272
I ate about 200 pistachios.

556
00:21:16,872 --> 00:21:19,112
So I shelled about 1,200 pistachios.

557
00:21:19,912 --> 00:21:21,982
I shelled more pistachios than a regular chipmunk.

558
00:21:22,282 --> 00:21:24,752
(Hang in there, Old Chip-Moo.)

559
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:29,952
(The pistachios are finally entering the oven.)

560
00:21:31,462 --> 00:21:33,021
Roast the oven for 5 to 7 minutes.

561
00:21:33,022 --> 00:21:34,302
(He finally activates the oven.)

562
00:21:35,192 --> 00:21:37,432
(Is it going well?)

563
00:21:38,532 --> 00:21:40,372
(Golden brown)

564
00:21:43,172 --> 00:21:44,442
(Amazed)

565
00:21:45,502 --> 00:21:47,471
It smells so nutty. Oh, my.

566
00:21:47,472 --> 00:21:49,112
Take them out carefully. Don't drop them.

567
00:21:50,442 --> 00:21:51,942
(Smelling)

568
00:21:52,082 --> 00:21:53,522
It smells so nutty. This is no joke.

569
00:21:54,012 --> 00:21:55,511
They smell different, right?

570
00:21:55,512 --> 00:21:58,152
- Definitely. They're much nuttier. - Does the smell get stronger?

571
00:22:02,552 --> 00:22:03,628
You're putting them up there again?

572
00:22:03,652 --> 00:22:05,862
Oh, no. I'm getting nervous. Why are you doing that?

573
00:22:05,962 --> 00:22:07,491
Why do you keep putting them there?

574
00:22:07,492 --> 00:22:08,562
(Why on earth?)

575
00:22:10,092 --> 00:22:12,131
- No! - No. Why are you doing that?

576
00:22:12,132 --> 00:22:13,301
Fine. Let them fall again.

577
00:22:13,302 --> 00:22:14,901
(Realizing his mistake)

578
00:22:14,902 --> 00:22:17,041
(Relieved)

579
00:22:17,042 --> 00:22:18,841
- I almost put it there. - This is hot.

580
00:22:18,842 --> 00:22:19,902
I must have lost my mind.

581
00:22:20,572 --> 00:22:23,242
It's so hot.

582
00:22:24,142 --> 00:22:25,311
It's hot.

583
00:22:25,312 --> 00:22:27,212
(The pistachios are safely delivered.)

584
00:22:27,812 --> 00:22:29,712
(Relieved)

585
00:22:31,052 --> 00:22:32,881
(Cool them for some now.)

586
00:22:32,882 --> 00:22:35,092
He hasn't even started on making the spread. Oh, no.

587
00:22:35,192 --> 00:22:36,552
I basically got nothing done.

588
00:22:38,722 --> 00:22:41,532
(Rummaging through)

589
00:22:42,762 --> 00:22:45,002
(He grabs something and comes to the kitchen.)

590
00:22:48,232 --> 00:22:49,902
That's done. All right.

591
00:22:50,602 --> 00:22:52,302
I need 600g of soft flour.

592
00:22:53,672 --> 00:22:54,941
I didn't know flour went into it.

593
00:22:54,942 --> 00:22:57,111
(He's adding soft flour after working on the pistachios?)

594
00:22:57,112 --> 00:22:58,142
Okay.

595
00:22:58,442 --> 00:22:59,511
I need 300g...

596
00:22:59,512 --> 00:23:00,551
- of corn starch. - That's 300g of corn starch.

597
00:23:00,552 --> 00:23:01,582
Write that down.

598
00:23:01,912 --> 00:23:02,952
So 600g and 300g.

599
00:23:03,352 --> 00:23:06,522
Next, I have to sift them?

600
00:23:12,622 --> 00:23:13,931
I did the same thing before,

601
00:23:13,932 --> 00:23:15,231
- and my sister scolded me a lot. - Darn it.

602
00:23:15,232 --> 00:23:16,838
(He's asking to get scolded for the flour mess.)

603
00:23:16,862 --> 00:23:17,862
Oh, no. His phone.

604
00:23:17,863 --> 00:23:18,902
(His phone falls victim.)

605
00:23:19,062 --> 00:23:20,432
When everyone watches this,

606
00:23:20,632 --> 00:23:22,032
the trend of DCC will be over.

607
00:23:22,932 --> 00:23:23,941
I didn't mean anything by it.

608
00:23:23,942 --> 00:23:25,841
- It's so annoying to watch. - Exactly.

609
00:23:25,842 --> 00:23:27,811
Out of all the recent videos I watched, this is...

610
00:23:27,812 --> 00:23:29,488
- the most annoying one. - It's so stressful.

611
00:23:29,512 --> 00:23:32,411
Watching him make the cookies should be fun and enjoyable.

612
00:23:32,412 --> 00:23:33,951
But why is his video making me so angry?

613
00:23:33,952 --> 00:23:36,352
- So it's a complicated recipe. - Right.

614
00:23:36,912 --> 00:23:38,981
(Making Moo chewy cookies is harder than one might think.)

615
00:23:38,982 --> 00:23:40,652
(But he's still trying his best.)

616
00:23:41,722 --> 00:23:44,822
(The flour is getting sifted finely thanks to his hard work.)

617
00:23:45,422 --> 00:23:47,592
I need 12g of white vinegar.

618
00:23:47,762 --> 00:23:49,632
White vinegar?

619
00:23:50,362 --> 00:23:53,201
I need 168g of canola oil.

620
00:23:53,202 --> 00:23:54,272
How fascinating.

621
00:23:55,072 --> 00:23:56,902
Then add cold water to them.

622
00:23:58,702 --> 00:24:00,911
"Stir well until it becomes watery."

623
00:24:00,912 --> 00:24:02,312
Then sift it again?

624
00:24:03,982 --> 00:24:05,541
Why isn't it turning liquid?

625
00:24:05,542 --> 00:24:07,412
Why is this... My gosh. Did it solidify?

626
00:24:08,212 --> 00:24:10,751
Out of everything I did today, I put the most care...

627
00:24:10,752 --> 00:24:12,451
into making kadayif.

628
00:24:12,452 --> 00:24:14,522
I'm making kadayif now.

629
00:24:15,522 --> 00:24:18,462
(The key ingredient of DCC, kadayif)

630
00:24:18,862 --> 00:24:21,391
- It's Middle Eastern noodles. - Now, I get it.

631
00:24:21,392 --> 00:24:22,392
They're so popular that...

632
00:24:22,392 --> 00:24:23,392
- it's impossible to buy them now. - Right.

633
00:24:23,393 --> 00:24:24,502
So I decided to make them.

634
00:24:24,802 --> 00:24:26,061
- You didn't use substitutes? - You decided to make them instead.

635
00:24:26,062 --> 00:24:27,401
- I didn't. - I see.

636
00:24:27,402 --> 00:24:28,471
- That's Moo-dayif. - Right.

637
00:24:28,472 --> 00:24:29,801
You made your own kadayif.

638
00:24:29,802 --> 00:24:31,802
Making the noodles will take a lot of time.

639
00:24:33,172 --> 00:24:34,341
(He starts making kadayif.)

640
00:24:34,342 --> 00:24:37,212
This is the thickness I need.

641
00:24:38,212 --> 00:24:39,682
It's watery. Nice.

642
00:24:40,282 --> 00:24:41,482
I think it will work.

643
00:24:42,552 --> 00:24:44,281
- It has to be watery? - Yes.

644
00:24:44,282 --> 00:24:46,182
- Right. - It must go into the piping bags.

645
00:24:46,522 --> 00:24:48,592
(He's sifting the batter one more time.)

646
00:24:48,792 --> 00:24:50,791
After watching this clip, I can now understand why...

647
00:24:50,792 --> 00:24:52,562
- Right. - they're so expensive.

648
00:24:53,392 --> 00:24:55,391
"Sift it and put it into a piping bag."

649
00:24:55,392 --> 00:24:57,132
"Make a hole with a needle."

650
00:24:57,432 --> 00:24:59,301
"Squeeze out very thin noodles on the pan..."

651
00:24:59,302 --> 00:25:01,131
- Right. - "and cook them."

652
00:25:01,132 --> 00:25:02,172
That's crazy.

653
00:25:02,972 --> 00:25:04,441
Only a needle should be able to pass through.

654
00:25:04,442 --> 00:25:06,282
If it's too big, they become kalguksu noodles.

655
00:25:06,442 --> 00:25:07,472
Then it's over.

656
00:25:08,672 --> 00:25:10,512
(Staring)

657
00:25:10,742 --> 00:25:12,682
It should be really tiny.

658
00:25:13,912 --> 00:25:16,082
(It can't get too big.)

659
00:25:16,782 --> 00:25:17,981
You can't mess up this part.

660
00:25:17,982 --> 00:25:19,722
Oh, my. You need to be delicate.

661
00:25:23,322 --> 00:25:24,391
(Careful)

662
00:25:24,392 --> 00:25:25,422
Hold tightly.

663
00:25:28,462 --> 00:25:31,862
(Make very thin noodles.)

664
00:25:34,472 --> 00:25:36,171
- This is how you do it? - Like that?

665
00:25:36,172 --> 00:25:37,872
- That's how you do it? - What?

666
00:25:37,942 --> 00:25:39,042
I'm making kadayif now.

667
00:25:39,642 --> 00:25:40,811
You're making them individually?

668
00:25:40,812 --> 00:25:42,572
- Of course. - It will take forever then.

669
00:25:42,742 --> 00:25:45,642
Do you know the sauce bottle for takoyaki?

670
00:25:45,982 --> 00:25:47,851
I saw bakers use the bottle to make kadayif.

671
00:25:47,852 --> 00:25:49,212
- Right. - Yes.

672
00:25:50,212 --> 00:25:52,852
(He's doing it the hard way when it can be much easier.)

673
00:25:53,182 --> 00:25:54,691
Come on. Darn it.

674
00:25:54,692 --> 00:25:57,022
(Squeezing out diligently)

675
00:25:57,792 --> 00:26:00,162
(Utterly focused)

676
00:26:01,732 --> 00:26:03,332
I'll stop here for now.

677
00:26:04,262 --> 00:26:05,931
- They look so different. - Oh, no.

678
00:26:05,932 --> 00:26:07,692
- It looks like mayonnaise. - They're thick.

679
00:26:07,802 --> 00:26:09,401
- The hole was quite big. - Was it?

680
00:26:09,402 --> 00:26:11,141
- They should've been thinner. - Right.

681
00:26:11,142 --> 00:26:13,782
- They should be as thin as needles. - They have to be super thin.

682
00:26:14,142 --> 00:26:15,142
What?

683
00:26:17,212 --> 00:26:19,582
(Scraping)

684
00:26:20,182 --> 00:26:22,582
(They're coming off, though.)

685
00:26:22,852 --> 00:26:26,021
(Chuckling)

686
00:26:26,022 --> 00:26:27,082
He's cute.

687
00:26:27,992 --> 00:26:29,091
It's working.

688
00:26:29,092 --> 00:26:30,251
- I get it now. - He gets it now.

689
00:26:30,252 --> 00:26:32,662
- He's getting the hang of it now. - My gosh.

690
00:26:34,362 --> 00:26:37,302
(He gives squeezing out the batter another try.)

691
00:26:39,902 --> 00:26:41,572
- You got it. - You're good.

692
00:26:43,532 --> 00:26:46,572
(The batter is coming out quite thinly now.)

693
00:26:48,642 --> 00:26:50,812
To make them crispy,

694
00:26:51,282 --> 00:26:54,082
I preheated the oven at 150°C...

695
00:26:54,452 --> 00:26:57,252
and roasted them for 5 to 7 minutes.

696
00:26:57,482 --> 00:26:59,951
On top of that, I removed all the moisture...

697
00:26:59,952 --> 00:27:01,452
and fried them with butter.

698
00:27:02,822 --> 00:27:04,892
You have to fry them with butter.

699
00:27:06,762 --> 00:27:08,232
This looks pretty legit.

700
00:27:09,692 --> 00:27:12,161
I was able to crisp them up.

701
00:27:12,162 --> 00:27:14,002
(He finally makes crispy and crunchy kadayif.)

702
00:27:15,402 --> 00:27:16,931
Pistachio and chocolate.

703
00:27:16,932 --> 00:27:18,542
(Next, he will make the filling.)

704
00:27:19,802 --> 00:27:21,641
Seriously. Making them requires a lot of work.

705
00:27:21,642 --> 00:27:23,372
Here it goes. I'll make the spread next.

706
00:27:27,282 --> 00:27:28,612
I need 60g of canola oil.

707
00:27:32,552 --> 00:27:33,552
Okay.

708
00:27:33,982 --> 00:27:34,992
It's 61g.

709
00:27:35,092 --> 00:27:36,252
Gosh, he's in trouble.

710
00:27:39,422 --> 00:27:40,761
I'll do half and half.

711
00:27:40,762 --> 00:27:41,968
(Filling it up with pistachios)

712
00:27:41,992 --> 00:27:43,061
I added too much.

713
00:27:43,062 --> 00:27:44,162
I'll only add half too.

714
00:27:44,302 --> 00:27:47,872
(Pouring the canola oil)

715
00:27:51,642 --> 00:27:53,441
(He pours out the next ingredient without hesitation.)

716
00:27:53,442 --> 00:27:56,872
I need 220g of white chocolate.

717
00:27:58,042 --> 00:27:59,311
(I need to add 70g more.)

718
00:27:59,312 --> 00:28:00,481
This is a lot of work.

719
00:28:00,482 --> 00:28:02,151
You used chocolate from Valrhona.

720
00:28:02,152 --> 00:28:03,311
- Is that a good brand? - Yes.

721
00:28:03,312 --> 00:28:04,622
I'm glad he knows that.

722
00:28:05,922 --> 00:28:06,981
Just have sweet cookies.

723
00:28:06,982 --> 00:28:09,152
(Forget about measuring.)

724
00:28:09,522 --> 00:28:12,421
(Adding 300g of chocolate to sweeten them up)

725
00:28:12,422 --> 00:28:13,721
I stopped measuring them.

726
00:28:13,722 --> 00:28:15,092
It was so annoying.

727
00:28:15,792 --> 00:28:17,801
(He puts the chocolate in the microwave.)

728
00:28:17,802 --> 00:28:19,262
It's fine. They'll just be sweet.

729
00:28:20,602 --> 00:28:23,071
Half a pinch of salt.

730
00:28:23,072 --> 00:28:25,642
(He follows his gut and adds more than half a pinch of salt.)

731
00:28:26,342 --> 00:28:28,912
(Placing the bottle, completely full, into the blender)

732
00:28:29,112 --> 00:28:31,042
- That startled me. - It will create some smoke.

733
00:28:32,182 --> 00:28:34,212
- The motor almost exploded. - Right. I bet.

734
00:28:35,352 --> 00:28:36,782
The color looks pretty legit.

735
00:28:37,552 --> 00:28:38,882
It's turning green.

736
00:28:40,682 --> 00:28:41,722
My gosh.

737
00:28:46,392 --> 00:28:48,832
(Gosh, it worked.)

738
00:28:50,332 --> 00:28:51,461
(After a boost of confidence...)

739
00:28:51,462 --> 00:28:53,002
White chocolate.

740
00:28:53,132 --> 00:28:55,032
The melted white chocolate from earlier.

741
00:28:55,202 --> 00:28:57,032
(Adding a spoonful of white chocolate)

742
00:28:57,942 --> 00:28:58,942
(Licking)

743
00:29:01,412 --> 00:29:02,442
It's amazing.

744
00:29:03,442 --> 00:29:04,581
(It looks similar to the store-bought pistachio spread.)

745
00:29:04,582 --> 00:29:05,642
It's perfect.

746
00:29:07,782 --> 00:29:09,852
(He takes out a new spoon.)

747
00:29:11,682 --> 00:29:13,051
I wonder how it tastes.

748
00:29:13,052 --> 00:29:14,572
- It's very savory. - Is it very rich?

749
00:29:16,452 --> 00:29:17,462
(Impressed)

750
00:29:19,392 --> 00:29:21,192
It's so tasty. It's just like spread.

751
00:29:22,232 --> 00:29:23,592
It'll be delicious on bread.

752
00:29:24,962 --> 00:29:26,232
It isn't too sweet. I love it.

753
00:29:27,632 --> 00:29:28,632
(Impressed)

754
00:29:28,802 --> 00:29:29,871
It's so thick.

755
00:29:29,872 --> 00:29:31,742
(That's what I'm talking about.)

756
00:29:33,572 --> 00:29:36,212
- It's thick and rich. - That's right. Rich.

757
00:29:37,982 --> 00:29:39,682
This spread is really tasty.

758
00:29:40,512 --> 00:29:42,782
(Mixing)

759
00:29:43,212 --> 00:29:44,381
It's perfect.

760
00:29:44,382 --> 00:29:46,521
(Kadayif and pistachio become one.)

761
00:29:46,522 --> 00:29:49,091
I'll begin the dung beetle's work in earnest.

762
00:29:49,092 --> 00:29:51,322
(I'll begin the dung beetle's work in earnest now.)

763
00:29:51,822 --> 00:29:54,792
(He puts on his gloves properly.)

764
00:29:55,692 --> 00:29:56,892
(Licking)

765
00:29:57,902 --> 00:29:59,702
(Scooping)

766
00:30:00,802 --> 00:30:02,771
- Experts call it... - Okay.

767
00:30:02,772 --> 00:30:04,812
- the dung beetle's work. - The dung beetle's work?

768
00:30:04,942 --> 00:30:05,972
The pieces are small.

769
00:30:06,472 --> 00:30:09,942
(Moo Dung Beetle puts it down gently.)

770
00:30:18,652 --> 00:30:20,152
What's wrong with it?

771
00:30:20,482 --> 00:30:21,752
There's nothing wrong with it.

772
00:30:21,992 --> 00:30:23,221
Why is it doing that?

773
00:30:23,222 --> 00:30:24,822
- I like it moist. - Is that so?

774
00:30:24,892 --> 00:30:27,092
I prefer the moist filling like that one.

775
00:30:27,422 --> 00:30:29,162
I think that one should be called wet.

776
00:30:29,662 --> 00:30:31,262
It'd be more appropriate to call it wet.

777
00:30:32,102 --> 00:30:33,662
(Glancing over)

778
00:30:36,732 --> 00:30:37,971
(It's pistachio filling, but...)

779
00:30:37,972 --> 00:30:39,372
Everyone, it's pistachio.

780
00:30:41,442 --> 00:30:42,642
Please don't misunderstand.

781
00:30:43,172 --> 00:30:45,641
It looks so unappetizing. For goodness' sake.

782
00:30:45,642 --> 00:30:46,642
(Oh, no)

783
00:30:46,643 --> 00:30:48,051
I can't believe how unappetizing it looks.

784
00:30:48,052 --> 00:30:49,181
(Moo Dung Beetle tries to salvage it...)

785
00:30:49,182 --> 00:30:50,452
(with his artistic soul.)

786
00:30:52,282 --> 00:30:53,351
(Fiddling)

787
00:30:53,352 --> 00:30:54,392
Tanzania.

788
00:30:56,222 --> 00:30:57,661
- Twelve. - What...

789
00:30:57,662 --> 00:30:58,791
(The 12 blobs seem to show the changing process.)

790
00:30:58,792 --> 00:30:59,892
What...

791
00:31:01,032 --> 00:31:03,032
What... What's that in the right corner?

792
00:31:03,762 --> 00:31:05,531
- There wasn't enough kadayif. - I see.

793
00:31:05,532 --> 00:31:06,602
I ran out.

794
00:31:08,032 --> 00:31:09,872
(Stretching)

795
00:31:12,072 --> 00:31:16,272
(He uses a plastic bag to cover the precious Moo chewy cookies.)

796
00:31:17,012 --> 00:31:20,682
(They go into the fridge.)

797
00:31:21,852 --> 00:31:23,382
(Sighing)

798
00:31:23,782 --> 00:31:24,882
My goodness.

799
00:31:26,252 --> 00:31:27,351
- They were dirty dishes. - My goodness.

800
00:31:27,352 --> 00:31:28,352
(A big mess)

801
00:31:30,492 --> 00:31:31,562
It looks so bad.

802
00:31:32,622 --> 00:31:33,822
It really looks like cow dung.

803
00:31:35,762 --> 00:31:37,162
How did you clean it up?

804
00:31:37,432 --> 00:31:38,701
Oh, no. He's spreading it.

805
00:31:38,702 --> 00:31:39,702
(Wiping)

806
00:31:39,703 --> 00:31:40,862
Gosh, this is...

807
00:31:42,002 --> 00:31:45,001
Since the ingredients were oil and chocolate,

808
00:31:45,002 --> 00:31:47,682
- it must've been hard to clean up. - That's right. The oil stains.

809
00:31:47,872 --> 00:31:49,212
(Wiping)

810
00:31:50,442 --> 00:31:52,281
(He wipes the traces from the nooks and crannies.)

811
00:31:52,282 --> 00:31:53,482
It really looks like dung.

812
00:31:54,312 --> 00:31:55,882
Gosh, it looks so bad.

813
00:31:56,582 --> 00:31:58,682
Why doesn't it look as good as it tastes?

814
00:31:59,522 --> 00:32:01,851
I briefly thought about throwing away the induction stove.

815
00:32:01,852 --> 00:32:04,252
(I briefly thought about throwing away the induction stove.)

816
00:32:05,562 --> 00:32:06,562
(My goodness)

817
00:32:08,362 --> 00:32:10,161
The puffiness is gone, Hyun Moo.

818
00:32:10,162 --> 00:32:11,861
- You know that I worked hard. - How did they come up with it?

819
00:32:11,862 --> 00:32:14,501
Must you do everything manually?

820
00:32:14,502 --> 00:32:16,131
- That's right. - Can't you use a machine?

821
00:32:16,132 --> 00:32:17,132
Everything is done manually.

822
00:32:17,133 --> 00:32:20,042
One peels pistachios, one kneads the dough,

823
00:32:20,242 --> 00:32:21,941
and one bakes the kadayif. The tasks are divided up.

824
00:32:21,942 --> 00:32:23,341
There's another person to score the ingredients.

825
00:32:23,342 --> 00:32:24,411
- That's right. - That's right.

826
00:32:24,412 --> 00:32:26,652
I shouldn't have looked down on the Dubai chewy cookies.

827
00:32:27,312 --> 00:32:29,672
- They live up to their fame. - They live up to their fame.

828
00:32:30,252 --> 00:32:31,721
It's time to make the wrapper.

829
00:32:31,722 --> 00:32:33,122
(It's time to make the wrapper.)

830
00:32:33,522 --> 00:32:34,522
Okay.

831
00:32:34,523 --> 00:32:35,622
(The current time is 11am.)

832
00:32:36,292 --> 00:32:38,461
This is 400g and I need 140g.

833
00:32:38,462 --> 00:32:39,692
(This is 400g of butter.)

834
00:32:40,862 --> 00:32:42,091
(Cutting)

835
00:32:42,092 --> 00:32:43,292
You didn't measure the amount.

836
00:32:43,532 --> 00:32:45,132
I stopped doing that at one point.

837
00:32:46,202 --> 00:32:47,462
It's exactly 140g.

838
00:32:48,172 --> 00:32:49,232
Okay.

839
00:32:50,332 --> 00:32:52,901
I need 60g of cocoa powder.

840
00:32:52,902 --> 00:32:56,612
(His goal is 60g of cocoa powder.)

841
00:32:57,242 --> 00:32:58,942
Five grams. Five grams are fine.

842
00:32:59,382 --> 00:33:00,582
It'll just get richer.

843
00:33:01,212 --> 00:33:02,351
And 30g of dry milk.

844
00:33:02,352 --> 00:33:04,151
(With 65g of cocoa powder and 30g of dry milk, )

845
00:33:04,152 --> 00:33:05,952
(his goal is a total of 95g.)

846
00:33:06,382 --> 00:33:07,382
All done.

847
00:33:07,383 --> 00:33:09,452
Once the marshmallows melt, I'll add them.

848
00:33:10,552 --> 00:33:12,562
(He picks up a bag of marshmallows.)

849
00:33:13,922 --> 00:33:14,931
- What? - What? My goodness.

850
00:33:14,932 --> 00:33:15,932
(He pours out the entire bag.)

851
00:33:15,933 --> 00:33:17,293
Are you supposed to add that much?

852
00:33:17,802 --> 00:33:20,202
(Without hesitation, he empties one more bag.)

853
00:33:21,232 --> 00:33:22,532
It'll be so fattening.

854
00:33:23,532 --> 00:33:25,492
I'll need to run around the globe about 15 times.

855
00:33:25,842 --> 00:33:26,842
Like this.

856
00:33:26,843 --> 00:33:28,571
Apparently, marshmallows are pretty low-calorie.

857
00:33:28,572 --> 00:33:29,572
- That's right. - That's right.

858
00:33:29,573 --> 00:33:32,111
- Marshmallows feel so victimized. - That's right.

859
00:33:32,112 --> 00:33:34,181
But it's best not to eat them if you can help it.

860
00:33:34,182 --> 00:33:35,252
Of course.

861
00:33:37,152 --> 00:33:38,222
It's melting.

862
00:33:38,452 --> 00:33:40,222
It's turning into marshmallow in Choco Pie.

863
00:33:41,952 --> 00:33:43,121
Around this time...

864
00:33:43,122 --> 00:33:44,442
(Let's see. Around this time...)

865
00:33:44,492 --> 00:33:45,761
You add cocoa powder.

866
00:33:45,762 --> 00:33:47,021
(Sprinkle the cocoa powder and dry milk.)

867
00:33:47,022 --> 00:33:48,962
- That's how you melt it. - That's right.

868
00:33:51,332 --> 00:33:53,732
(Mix well with the melted marshmallows.)

869
00:33:54,362 --> 00:33:56,572
It's soft like my belly.

870
00:34:02,742 --> 00:34:04,511
(He's carefree after making a mean comment.)

871
00:34:04,512 --> 00:34:05,582
These punks look cute.

872
00:34:06,582 --> 00:34:07,912
It took on one color.

873
00:34:08,582 --> 00:34:10,912
- You did a good job. - Right? It looks nice.

874
00:34:11,822 --> 00:34:12,822
All done.

875
00:34:12,823 --> 00:34:14,222
(Fascinated)

876
00:34:14,852 --> 00:34:15,852
Like this.

877
00:34:15,853 --> 00:34:17,068
(The marshmallow wrapper gets cooled down for a moment.)

878
00:34:17,092 --> 00:34:18,492
Now comes the final step.

879
00:34:19,392 --> 00:34:20,892
There are two versions.

880
00:34:21,962 --> 00:34:24,291
A must-have item for the Gen Zers, matcha.

881
00:34:24,292 --> 00:34:25,461
Matcha.

882
00:34:25,462 --> 00:34:26,761
I'll finish off matcha as well.

883
00:34:26,762 --> 00:34:28,502
(His eyes are crazed.)

884
00:34:29,402 --> 00:34:31,501
Cocoa powder and... Very nice.

885
00:34:31,502 --> 00:34:33,382
- Do you roll them on the top? - You roll them.

886
00:34:33,472 --> 00:34:35,311
- They take so much effort. - Seriously.

887
00:34:35,312 --> 00:34:36,511
Are they cooled down?

888
00:34:36,512 --> 00:34:38,592
(He checks up on the spread he cooled down earlier.)

889
00:34:39,542 --> 00:34:40,642
Ta-da.

890
00:34:41,042 --> 00:34:42,152
(Oh, my)

891
00:34:43,552 --> 00:34:45,222
Good. They look perfect.

892
00:34:45,352 --> 00:34:46,782
"They look perfect?"

893
00:34:47,182 --> 00:34:48,752
(Grinning)

894
00:34:50,022 --> 00:34:51,291
(Grabbing)

895
00:34:51,292 --> 00:34:52,292
It hardened.

896
00:34:54,392 --> 00:34:55,532
What do you do with that?

897
00:34:57,092 --> 00:34:59,902
(He shapes the hardened spread.)

898
00:35:00,402 --> 00:35:02,722
This is the dung beetle's work they talk about on YouTube.

899
00:35:03,732 --> 00:35:05,872
(Rolling)

900
00:35:06,402 --> 00:35:07,502
It smells so savory.

901
00:35:07,942 --> 00:35:09,311
(Impressed)

902
00:35:09,312 --> 00:35:11,211
- It must be delicious. - I must be a genius.

903
00:35:11,212 --> 00:35:12,881
(Oh, my!)

904
00:35:12,882 --> 00:35:13,912
I see...

905
00:35:14,412 --> 00:35:15,481
that it can be done.

906
00:35:15,482 --> 00:35:17,282
(He gains confidence and shapes the filling.)

907
00:35:17,612 --> 00:35:19,921
- It's too big like a fat macaron. - It's big-sized.

908
00:35:19,922 --> 00:35:22,122
- It should cost 40 dollars. - Did you say 40 dollars?

909
00:35:23,822 --> 00:35:25,622
This one doesn't have the crunchy ingredient.

910
00:35:26,562 --> 00:35:27,592
It's a dud.

911
00:35:28,292 --> 00:35:29,431
If you eat it, it's a dud.

912
00:35:29,432 --> 00:35:30,491
(Laughing)

913
00:35:30,492 --> 00:35:31,532
There isn't much kadayif.

914
00:35:32,162 --> 00:35:33,632
Why is it so small?

915
00:35:34,062 --> 00:35:35,932
I couldn't adjust the size.

916
00:35:36,272 --> 00:35:37,501
Couldn't you pull some out?

917
00:35:37,502 --> 00:35:39,501
- Two duds. - I was too exhausted.

918
00:35:39,502 --> 00:35:41,442
(He lacked the stamina.)

919
00:35:42,042 --> 00:35:43,072
It's too big.

920
00:35:43,372 --> 00:35:45,653
A Dubai chewy cookie of this size should cost 50 dollars.

921
00:35:46,312 --> 00:35:48,282
All of them are shaped differently.

922
00:35:49,952 --> 00:35:51,111
(He puts on new gloves when the spread is ready.)

923
00:35:51,112 --> 00:35:52,282
He's very neat.

924
00:35:53,182 --> 00:35:55,352
(Grabbing)

925
00:35:56,092 --> 00:35:57,421
(He pours oil over the new gloves.)

926
00:35:57,422 --> 00:35:58,691
Does it prevent sticking?

927
00:35:58,692 --> 00:36:00,212
It prevents marshmallow from sticking.

928
00:36:02,392 --> 00:36:04,061
(It's time to cover the filling with the marshmallow wrapper.)

929
00:36:04,062 --> 00:36:05,402
It must be hard to wrap it.

930
00:36:07,432 --> 00:36:08,731
- It looks like hotteok. - That's right.

931
00:36:08,732 --> 00:36:09,732
Right?

932
00:36:09,733 --> 00:36:11,572
- I bet it feels nice. - Yes, it feels nice.

933
00:36:12,542 --> 00:36:15,412
(He adds the spread inside the broad wrapper.)

934
00:36:16,312 --> 00:36:17,741
The wrapper is too thick.

935
00:36:17,742 --> 00:36:18,882
Hold on.

936
00:36:19,482 --> 00:36:20,942
- How will you... - What's that?

937
00:36:21,182 --> 00:36:22,281
How will you eat it?

938
00:36:22,282 --> 00:36:23,282
(My goodness)

939
00:36:23,283 --> 00:36:24,981
That's my aesthetic goal.

940
00:36:24,982 --> 00:36:28,222
I like Dubai chewy cookies that are excessive in every way.

941
00:36:28,622 --> 00:36:30,692
The wrapper should be thick...

942
00:36:30,792 --> 00:36:33,092
like a big dumpling.

943
00:36:33,592 --> 00:36:34,862
It needs to be chewy.

944
00:36:35,432 --> 00:36:38,501
As for kadayif, it should give the texture...

945
00:36:38,502 --> 00:36:40,402
of munching on sand.

946
00:36:40,562 --> 00:36:42,872
It should be crunchy enough to break the teeth.

947
00:36:43,072 --> 00:36:44,642
That's the texture I want.

948
00:36:45,342 --> 00:36:46,771
(He breaks free from the normal amount.)

949
00:36:46,772 --> 00:36:47,842
It looks nice.

950
00:36:48,872 --> 00:36:49,872
Okay.

951
00:36:49,873 --> 00:36:51,913
(Moo chewy cookies aren't stingy with ingredients.)

952
00:36:52,342 --> 00:36:54,441
Why... Hold on.

953
00:36:54,442 --> 00:36:55,482
(Hold on. Is it too big?)

954
00:36:56,482 --> 00:36:59,882
(He takes off some marshmallows.)

955
00:37:01,722 --> 00:37:02,822
It's still big.

956
00:37:03,392 --> 00:37:05,191
- I should use a bit at a time. - Is the tasty wrapper...

957
00:37:05,192 --> 00:37:06,321
thick or thin?

958
00:37:06,322 --> 00:37:07,661
These days, the thin wrapper is...

959
00:37:07,662 --> 00:37:08,732
- Is it the trend? - Yes.

960
00:37:09,332 --> 00:37:10,562
Let's make it smaller.

961
00:37:12,062 --> 00:37:13,102
How about this much?

962
00:37:14,062 --> 00:37:15,072
Thin.

963
00:37:16,932 --> 00:37:20,002
(He spreads it out thinly to follow the current trend.)

964
00:37:20,642 --> 00:37:22,442
It's the size we're familiar with.

965
00:37:26,442 --> 00:37:27,811
I can use a small amount.

966
00:37:27,812 --> 00:37:29,412
(He improves through trials and errors.)

967
00:37:29,582 --> 00:37:32,122
As thin as the xiaolongbao wrapper.

968
00:37:33,582 --> 00:37:34,622
Moo chewy cookies.

969
00:37:35,822 --> 00:37:39,061
(Sinking)

970
00:37:39,062 --> 00:37:40,062
Seriously.

971
00:37:41,462 --> 00:37:42,591
Fat chewy cookie.

972
00:37:42,592 --> 00:37:44,262
(He enjoys making Moo chewy cookies.)

973
00:37:45,262 --> 00:37:46,302
Fat.

974
00:37:47,732 --> 00:37:49,202
Thinly. One,

975
00:37:49,872 --> 00:37:51,232
two, three.

976
00:37:52,372 --> 00:37:53,502
(Ta-da!)

977
00:37:54,242 --> 00:37:56,071
- He suddenly became excited. - It was exciting.

978
00:37:56,072 --> 00:37:57,471
(The tactile play excites Child Moo.)

979
00:37:57,472 --> 00:37:58,482
Okay.

980
00:37:59,642 --> 00:38:00,982
Anger disappeared from his face.

981
00:38:02,212 --> 00:38:03,652
This one is like potato rice cake.

982
00:38:04,052 --> 00:38:05,082
It's big.

983
00:38:06,482 --> 00:38:08,752
This one is like musubi. Mu chewy cookie.

984
00:38:09,992 --> 00:38:12,122
This one is like a dumpling. Dump chewy cookie.

985
00:38:13,392 --> 00:38:14,592
He's coming up with new words.

986
00:38:15,392 --> 00:38:17,262
Two... There's no more. I'm in trouble.

987
00:38:17,932 --> 00:38:19,062
I ran out.

988
00:38:19,162 --> 00:38:20,362
- What? - What do you do then?

989
00:38:26,602 --> 00:38:28,042
You should sacrifice yourself.

990
00:38:30,072 --> 00:38:32,282
(He collects marshmallow from the ready-made ones.)

991
00:38:32,612 --> 00:38:33,842
What should I do with this?

992
00:38:35,682 --> 00:38:37,182
The filling is visible in this one.

993
00:38:37,412 --> 00:38:39,092
This one is like an inside-out sushi roll.

994
00:38:39,522 --> 00:38:40,882
This hasn't been attempted before.

995
00:38:41,892 --> 00:38:43,391
- Oh, my. - My goodness.

996
00:38:43,392 --> 00:38:44,451
Inside-out Dubai chewy cookie.

997
00:38:44,452 --> 00:38:46,962
- My goodness. - Gosh.

998
00:38:47,122 --> 00:38:48,631
(They can't help but groan.)

999
00:38:48,632 --> 00:38:49,692
Gosh, that's...

1000
00:38:50,062 --> 00:38:51,532
- I can't believe it. - Gosh.

1001
00:38:51,732 --> 00:38:52,761
Cow dung cookie.

1002
00:38:52,762 --> 00:38:53,762
(What do you think?)

1003
00:38:54,602 --> 00:38:57,432
I'm beginning to shape them nicely instead of playing around.

1004
00:39:00,802 --> 00:39:03,211
I'll cover the burst areas with the powder.

1005
00:39:03,212 --> 00:39:05,252
They'll look fine once the burst areas are covered.

1006
00:39:05,382 --> 00:39:07,582
(He applies the cocoa and matcha powder evenly.)

1007
00:39:08,952 --> 00:39:10,012
Good.

1008
00:39:11,882 --> 00:39:13,821
- Don't they look nice? - I can see that they're handmade.

1009
00:39:13,822 --> 00:39:15,092
- Not bad. - You did well.

1010
00:39:15,622 --> 00:39:17,351
- My goodness. - My goodness.

1011
00:39:17,352 --> 00:39:19,712
(Store-bought Dubai chewy cookies versus Moo chewy cookies)

1012
00:39:19,762 --> 00:39:20,861
Did it take five hours?

1013
00:39:20,862 --> 00:39:22,031
- It's past 12pm. - Okay.

1014
00:39:22,032 --> 00:39:23,132
It's lunchtime.

1015
00:39:23,692 --> 00:39:25,502
Shall I package them now?

1016
00:39:27,062 --> 00:39:29,832
(He bought cases for Dubai chewy cookies.)

1017
00:39:30,332 --> 00:39:32,002
They won't fit since they're so big.

1018
00:39:36,842 --> 00:39:38,782
It's pretty.

1019
00:39:43,912 --> 00:39:45,022
What's this?

1020
00:39:45,822 --> 00:39:47,222
For goodness' sake.

1021
00:39:48,192 --> 00:39:49,592
- What? - Isn't it upside down?

1022
00:39:50,252 --> 00:39:52,592
- That's the bottom side. - I didn't know.

1023
00:39:52,822 --> 00:39:53,822
For goodness' sake.

1024
00:39:53,823 --> 00:39:56,092
(Is it a faulty product?)

1025
00:39:57,692 --> 00:39:58,802
It looks strange.

1026
00:40:00,432 --> 00:40:01,902
- It's a random box. - Yes.

1027
00:40:02,032 --> 00:40:04,072
- It's like a draw. - It keeps you excited.

1028
00:40:04,732 --> 00:40:06,742
(He continues to wrap them secretively.)

1029
00:40:07,702 --> 00:40:08,942
I won't be able to close it.

1030
00:40:09,612 --> 00:40:10,672
It's too big.

1031
00:40:12,482 --> 00:40:14,642
- I love how he broke the mold. - Yes.

1032
00:40:15,182 --> 00:40:16,252
What should I do?

1033
00:40:17,912 --> 00:40:19,852
The shape should be pretty.

1034
00:40:22,122 --> 00:40:23,852
Isn't this more hip?

1035
00:40:27,692 --> 00:40:28,732
It's hip.

1036
00:40:30,692 --> 00:40:32,162
It looks very valuable.

1037
00:40:32,302 --> 00:40:33,831
- Doesn't it look hip? - It looks like...

1038
00:40:33,832 --> 00:40:35,172
that dog thing on the street.

1039
00:40:35,932 --> 00:40:37,402
- Poop bag? - Poop bag.

1040
00:40:37,702 --> 00:40:38,742
Let's go.

1041
00:40:39,302 --> 00:40:41,842
(Dog poop cookie)

1042
00:40:42,012 --> 00:40:43,172
It's like a special treat.

1043
00:40:44,442 --> 00:40:45,882
It looks like a hard-to-get item.

1044
00:40:46,312 --> 00:40:48,558
Break free from the mold of packaging everything prettily.

1045
00:40:48,582 --> 00:40:50,852
